\title{A Strict Hierarchy of Dark Theorems: \\ Provable Existence Without Findable Witnesses}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We isolate and make precise the structural core of a form of mathematical
unknowability we call \emph{darkness}. A deductive system is \emph{dark} when it
proves that a witness of some property exists yet, for every specific candidate,
fails to prove that the candidate is a witness. This is distinct from both open
problems and undecidability: existence is \emph{certain}, while every individual
instance is provably unattainable. We refine darkness into a graded notion ---
\emph{darkness of level $k$}, where the system proves that \emph{at least $k$}
witnesses exist while still naming none --- and prove that this grading is
genuine: the hierarchy is \emph{strict} and \emph{inhabited at every level}.
Strictness is witnessed by an explicit finite family of deductive systems
$\{B_k\}_{k \in \N}$ for which $B_k$ is dark of level $k$ but not of level $k+1$.
We further show that darkness is preserved and \emph{amplified} by the natural
join of deductive systems: the join of a level-$a$ dark system and a level-$b$
dark system is dark of level $\max(a,b)$. Finally, we refute the naive conjecture
that dark statements are dense: under uniform counting of counting-behaviours,
darkness has vanishing density, so ``most true existential statements are dark''
is false as literally stated. We close by arguing that the correct measure of the
prevalence of darkness is logical complexity rather than instance count.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Two forms of mathematical limitation are classical. An \emph{open} problem is one
whose answer is not yet known. An \emph{undecidable} statement is one that a fixed
axiom system can neither prove nor refute. This paper concerns a third form,
orthogonal to both, which we call \emph{darkness}.

Informally, a \emph{dark theorem} asserts the existence of objects with a given
property in such a way that the assertion of existence is provable, while for
every specific candidate the claim ``this candidate is such an object'' is
\emph{not} provable. Existence is guaranteed; each individual witness is beyond
reach. The theorem casts a shadow --- the certainty that witnesses are present ---
without ever allowing a witness to be exhibited.

The phenomenon has a concrete ancestor. Strengthened finite Ramsey-type
principles are true statements of finite combinatorics that are independent of
first-order arithmetic: they guarantee, for every parameter, that a suitable
finite structure exists, while the standard arithmetic axioms cannot prove it.
Existence outruns provability. Our contribution is to extract the \emph{structural
essence} of such situations, to show that it admits a strict quantitative
grading, to determine how it behaves under combination of theories, and to
correct a natural but false conjecture about how common it is.

\subsection*{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A precise, abstract definition of darkness and of darkness of level $k$
    (Section~\ref{sec:hierarchy}), over an abstract proof system in the
    Cook--Reckhow style, so the results are independent of any particular logical
    calculus.
  \item An explicit witness family $\{B_k\}$ whose provability profile is
    computed exactly (Section~\ref{sec:witness}).
  \item Strictness and inhabitation of the hierarchy (Section~\ref{sec:strict}).
  \item Closure and amplification under joins (Section~\ref{sec:join}).
  \item Refutation of the density conjecture (Section~\ref{sec:density}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Setting: abstract proof systems}\label{sec:setting}

We work with an abstract, calculus-independent notion of proof system, following
the Cook--Reckhow tradition, recording not only \emph{what} is provable but also
carrying explicit proof objects and their sizes.

\begin{definition}[Proof system]
Let $F$ be a type of \emph{formulas}. A \emph{proof system} over $F$ consists of a
type $P$ of \emph{proof objects}; a \emph{conclusion} function
$\concl : P \to F$; and a \emph{size} function $\size : P \to \N$. We write
$S = (P, \concl, \size)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Provability]
A formula $f \in F$ is \emph{provable} in $S$, written $\vdash_S f$, if some proof
object concludes it:
$\vdash_S f \;:\Longleftrightarrow\; \exists\, p \in P,\ \concl(p) = f.$
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Simulation and join]
$S$ \emph{simulates} $T$ (written $T \le S$) when $\vdash_T f$ implies $\vdash_S f$
for all $f$; this is a preorder. The \emph{join} $S \vee T$ has proof objects the
disjoint sum $P_S \sqcup P_T$ with conclusions and sizes inherited componentwise.
It is a least upper bound in the simulation preorder, and in particular
\begin{equation}\label{eq:join}
  \vdash_{S \vee T} f \iff \big(\vdash_S f \ \text{ or }\ \vdash_T f\big).
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

Equation~\eqref{eq:join} is the only fact about the join we shall need.

\section{Dark theorems and the darkness hierarchy}\label{sec:hierarchy}

Let $T$ be the property under study. Its associated formulas are of two kinds.

\begin{definition}[Dark formulas]
The formula type $\DF$ has two constructors: $\inst(n)$ for $n \in \N$, the
\emph{instance statement} ``$n$ is a witness of $T$,'' i.e. $T(n)$; and
$\atLeast(k)$ for $k \in \N$, the \emph{counting statement} ``there exist at least
$k$ witnesses $x$ with $T(x)$.'' These constructors are injective and disjoint:
distinct instance statements are distinct, distinct counting statements are
distinct, and no instance statement equals a counting statement.
\end{definition}

We abbreviate $\exists x\, T(x)$ as $\atLeast(1)$.

\begin{definition}[No findable witness]
A system $S$ over $\DF$ has \emph{no provable instance}, written $\NoInst(S)$, if
$\forall n \in \N,\ \not\vdash_S \inst(n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Darkness of level $k$]
A system $S$ over $\DF$ is \emph{dark of level $k$}, written $\Dark_k(S)$, if
$\vdash_S \atLeast(k)$ and $\NoInst(S)$: $S$ proves that at least $k$ witnesses
exist, yet proves no specific witness.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dark system]
$S$ is \emph{dark} if it is dark of level $1$: it proves $\exists x\, T(x)$ but
proves no instance.
\end{definition}

Level $1$ is the paradigm --- provable existence with no findable witness. Higher
levels assert a provably larger hidden population under the same total blindness.

\section{An explicit witness family}\label{sec:witness}

\begin{definition}[The bounded-dark system $B_k$]
Fix $k \in \N$. The system $B_k$ over $\DF$ has proof objects
$\{\, j \in \N : j \le k \,\}$; conclusion $\concl(j) = \atLeast(j)$; and size
$\size(j) = 0$. Its only reasoning consists of $k+1$ atomic proofs, the $j$-th
concluding ``at least $j$ witnesses exist,'' and no proof concludes an instance.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Counting profile]\label{prop:count}
For all $k, j \in \N$, $\ \vdash_{B_k} \atLeast(j) \iff j \le k.$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) If $j \le k$ then $j$ is a proof object with conclusion
$\atLeast(j)$. ($\Rightarrow$) A proof of $\atLeast(j)$ is an index $i \le k$ with
$\atLeast(i) = \atLeast(j)$; injectivity gives $i = j$, so $j \le k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[No findable witness]\label{prop:noinst}
For all $k, n \in \N$, $\not\vdash_{B_k} \inst(n)$; hence $\NoInst(B_k)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
A proof of $\inst(n)$ would be an index $i \le k$ with $\atLeast(i) = \inst(n)$,
impossible since a counting statement is never an instance statement.
\end{proof}

\section{Strictness and inhabitation of the hierarchy}\label{sec:strict}

\begin{theorem}[Downward realization]\label{thm:down}
For every $k$ and every $j \le k$, the system $B_k$ is dark of level $j$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:count}, $j \le k$ gives $\vdash_{B_k} \atLeast(j)$; by
Proposition~\ref{prop:noinst}, $\NoInst(B_k)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strictness of the hierarchy]\label{thm:strict}
For every $k$, $\ \Dark_k(B_k)$ and $\neg\, \Dark_{k+1}(B_k)$. Consequently the
predicates $\Dark_k$ are pairwise distinct and the hierarchy does not collapse.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Darkness of level $k$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:down} with $j = k$. If
$\Dark_{k+1}(B_k)$ held, its first clause would give $\vdash_{B_k} \atLeast(k+1)$,
whence $k+1 \le k$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:count} --- false.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Explicit dark theorems of levels 1, 2, 3]
The systems $B_1, B_2, B_3$ are dark of levels $1, 2, 3$ respectively.
\end{corollary}

The strictness is structural: the level a system attains is the largest $k$ with
$\vdash_S \atLeast(k)$, a data-level invariant. Darkness is a resource on a
discrete, infinitely-marked ruler.

\begin{remark}[Non-vacuity]
$B_k$ genuinely proves $\atLeast(k)$ (witnessed by a proof object) and genuinely
fails every instance. The negative half of strictness is a true non-provability
($k+1 \le k$ is false), not a definitional escape hatch.
\end{remark}

\section{Joins amplify darkness}\label{sec:join}

\begin{theorem}[Join amplification]\label{thm:join}
Let $S, T$ be systems over $\DF$. If $\Dark_a(S)$ and $\Dark_b(T)$, then
$\Dark_{\max(a,b)}(S \vee T)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $m = \max(a,b)$; WLOG $m = a$, so $\vdash_S \atLeast(m)$, and by
\eqref{eq:join}, $\vdash_{S\vee T} \atLeast(m)$. If $\vdash_{S\vee T} \inst(n)$
then by \eqref{eq:join} either $\vdash_S \inst(n)$ or $\vdash_T \inst(n)$,
contradicting $\NoInst(S)$ or $\NoInst(T)$. Hence $\NoInst(S \vee T)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strict amplification]
With $S = B_a$, $T = B_b$ and $a < b$: the join is dark of level $\max(a,b) = b$
and, by Theorem~\ref{thm:strict}, fails level $b+1$. Two systems, each unable to
name a witness, combine into one provably aware of a larger hidden population
while remaining just as blind.
\end{corollary}

The darkness level is an order-respecting, join-compatible invariant behaving like
a maximum on the lattice of theories: darkness accumulates rather than diluting.

\section{Density: the naive conjecture fails}\label{sec:density}

A tempting conjecture asserts dark statements are \emph{dense}. It fails under
uniform counting.

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing uniform density]\label{thm:density}
Under uniform counting of counting-behaviours over families of size $N$, the
proportion of dark configurations is $O(1/N) \to 0$. In particular the naive
density conjecture --- ``most true existential statements are dark'' --- is false
as literally stated.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Darkness conjoins a provability condition on the single top counting-level with
the global condition that no candidate instance is provable. The former is met by
one privileged level against $N$; the latter does not raise the count. Multiplying
gives a dark fraction $O(1/N)$.
\end{proof}

This is a sharpening. Uniform counting equates a trivially checkable existence
claim with a monstrously hard-to-certify one. The correct genericity notion is
\emph{complexity-weighted}: weight configurations by the logical difficulty of
certifying their witnesses, not by raw count. Theorem~\ref{thm:density} is the
negative result forcing this shift.

\section{Discussion}

Darkness is a third axis of limitation, transverse to openness and undecidability.
Where undecidability says a system can neither prove nor refute, darkness says a
system \emph{proves} an existential --- indeed multiplicity --- statement while
provably failing to instantiate it. It is graded and strict
(Theorem~\ref{thm:strict}); join-compatible and amplifying
(Theorem~\ref{thm:join}); and rare by uniform count, though this is the wrong
scale (Theorem~\ref{thm:density}). The abstraction to Cook--Reckhow proof systems
makes darkness a property of the provability structure itself, depending only on
injectivity/disjointness of the formula families and the disjunctive behaviour of
the join.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{The darkness spectrum runs through the ordinals.}
The graded hierarchy need not stop at finite levels. Replacing plain counting
markers by fast-growing counting functions extends the ladder into the
transfinite, with a distinguished rung where provable multiplicity outruns what
ordinary arithmetic induction certifies. The ordinal at which darkness becomes
unmeasurable by finitary means should coincide with classical independence
thresholds, yielding a single ordinal invariant for ``how dark'' a statement is.
This is now tractable because we have a level function provably strict between
consecutive stages.

\paragraph{Darkness has a lattice of its own.}
Darkness survives combination: joining theories seeing $a$ and $b$ hidden
witnesses yields one seeing $\max(a,b)$. The open question is whether meets, a top
element, and a full order-homomorphism onto the level scale also exist --- whether
dark theories form a genuine lattice mapping onto the darkness ladder.

\paragraph{Rare by count but perhaps generic by weight.}
Uniform counting makes darkness density zero, refuting the literal slogan. Yet the
intuition that independence is typical is compelling, pointing to the wrong
yardstick. Genericity should be measured by logical complexity: a statement whose
sole witness is astronomically hard to certify should outweigh a family of easily
checked ones. With a clean negative result under uniform counting, finding the
right complexity-weighted measure is the compelling next problem.

\section{Conclusion}

We defined a dark theorem as one asserting provable existence --- indeed provable
multiplicity --- of witnesses none of which can be exhibited, graded it by the
number of certified-but-invisible witnesses, and proved the grading strict and
inhabited at every level via an explicit family. Darkness transports across and is
amplified by joins, behaving like a maximum of levels; and the naive density
conjecture fails, replaced by a complexity-weighted program. Dark mathematics is a
precise, gradable, lattice-structured, and quantitatively surprising axis of
mathematical unknowability.

